module Darklang.Cli.Docs.SyntaxAtAGlance

/// How Dark writes the things a newcomer writes most. It opens `docs for-ai` and
/// `docs scripts`, so a reader who stops early has still seen it.
let content(): String =
  """## Dark syntax at a glance

  Stdlib.String.trim(s)   add(a, b)   main()   calls take parentheses and commas
  "n = " + Stdlib.display(n)                  `+` joins Strings
  2   -7   1.5   255u8   3s32   10z   9.5dec   a plain integer is an Int64
  [1, 2, 3]   Point { x = 1, y = 2 }           commas, or one item per line
  [x, ...xs]   [...xs, ...ys]                  prepend an item; join two lists
  Stdlib.List.append(xs, x)                    add one item at the end
  Stdlib.List.map(xs, fun x -> x + 1)          the list comes first
  Ok(n)   Error(e)   Some(x)   None            fields go in parentheses
  | Ok(n) -> ...   | [x, ...rest] -> ...       the same shapes in a match
  type Shape = | Dot | Circle(Float) | Rect(Float, Float)
  let f(p: String * Int64): Option<String * Int64> = Some(p)   tuple types use `*`
  let down(n: Int64): Int64 = if n <= 0 then 0 else down(n - 1)

A function calls itself by name; there is no `rec` keyword. Each statement goes
on its own line, and every line of a block but the last returns Unit. Name your
own types apart from the built-in ones (`Unit`, `String`, `Int64`, ...).

`docs syntax` has the full rules.
"""
